WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.020
Here is a settlement job, written the usual way.

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:16.601
One loop reads the clock, loads payments, skips refunds, holds recent ones, sums per merchant, computes fees, calls the payout API, and saves the ledger.

3
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.418
It works.

4
00:00:17.418 --> 00:00:25.731
But to test one fee rule, you need a fixed clock, a fake store, a fake payout API, and checks on what the fakes recorded.

5
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:27.264
Look at the colours.

6
00:00:27.264 --> 00:00:29.061
The teal lines only decide.

7
00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:31.124
The gold lines touch the world.

8
00:00:31.124 --> 00:00:36.115
Pull them apart: the decisions go into a core, and the I/O wraps around it.

9
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:37.964
The core is a pure function.

10
00:00:37.964 --> 00:00:44.328
It takes plain values, today's date included, and returns a plan: a value that says what should happen.

11
00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:48.221
This shape is called a functional core and an imperative shell.

12
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:51.079
Here is the smallest one, from the lesson.

13
00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.502
Card authorizations older than seven days get voided.

14
00:00:54.502 --> 00:01:00.120
The shell reads the clock, and loads two: a1, eight days old, and a2, thirty hours old.

15
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.948
It hands them to the core as values.

16
00:01:02.948 --> 00:01:08.393
The core returns two decisions: void a1, and keep a2, with 138 hours left.

17
00:01:08.393 --> 00:01:11.115
It called nothing, and read no clock.

18
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.848
Then the shell carries out the plan: one call to the gateway.

19
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.250
A test needs none of the shell.

20
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.603
Call the core with a list and a date, one day later, and compare the result.

21
00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:25.561
a2 is still kept, now with 114 hours left.

22
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:28.917
All of this needs inputs that hold still.

23
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:31.280
A read-only List does not promise that.

24
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.520
Build a batch from the caller's list, and both point at the same list.

25
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.158
The caller adds p2.

26
00:01:37.158 --> 00:01:41.303
The batch shows p1 and p2: it changed under your feet.

27
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.038
Copy at the boundary.

28
00:01:43.038 --> 00:01:48.895
With toList in Kotlin, or List.copyOf in Java, the batch keeps its own snapshot.

29
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.531
In code, the shell is three steps: read, decide, act.

30
00:01:53.531 --> 00:01:55.772
The same in Kotlin and in Java.

31
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.492
So: the core decides from plain values, and the shell does the I/O around it.

32
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:03.701
A quick check.

33
00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:06.774
A teammate reads the clock inside the core.

34
00:02:06.774 --> 00:02:07.989
Is it still pure?

35
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:10.599
No.

36
00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:15.718
The clock is a hidden input, so the same arguments can give a different plan.

37
00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:18.377
Read it in the shell, and pass today in.
